## Modifying Item Body Content

Use `lif update` to modify body content along with metadata changes:

**Replace text (exact match, must occur exactly once):**
```bash
lif update <id> --body-replace-old "- [ ] Task 1" --body-replace-new "- [x] Task 1"
```
- Errors if text not found or found multiple times
- Use empty string to delete the matched text

**Append content:**
```bash
lif update <id> --body-append "## Notes\n\nAdded content"
echo "Multi-line content" | lif update <id> --body-append -
```
- Adds text to end of body with blank line separator
- Use `-` to read from stdin

**Combined with metadata changes:**
```bash
lif update <id> \
  --body-replace-old "- [ ] Deploy to prod" --body-replace-new "- [x] Deploy to prod" \
  --status completed
```

**Multiple replacements (via GraphQL):**
```bash
lif graphql 'mutation {
  updateItem(id: "<id>", input: {
    status: "completed"
    bodyMod: {
      replace: [
        { old: "- [ ] Task 1", new: "- [x] Task 1" }
        { old: "- [ ] Task 2", new: "- [x] Task 2" }
      ]
      append: "## Summary\n\nAll tasks completed!"
    }
  }) { id body etag }
}'
```
- Replacements execute sequentially (each operates on result of previous)
- Append applied after all replacements
- All operations atomic with single etag validation
- Transactional: any failure = no changes saved

## Custom Front Matter

Items preserve front matter keys outside the known schema (title, status, type, priority, tags, parent, blocking, blocked_by, order) instead of dropping them. Use this for project-specific metadata that doesn't need its own flag -- a release cut, a classification, anything the built-in fields don't cover.

```bash
# Set one or more extra keys (repeatable, works on create and update)
lif create --json "Title" -t task --set release=0-5-0 --set klasse=bugfix
lif update --json <id> --set release=0-6-0

# Remove an extra key
lif update --json <id> --unset release

# Filter by an extra key (AND logic across repeats)
lif list --json --where release=0-5-0
```

- A key that names a known field (e.g. `--set status=done`) is rejected -- use the native flag (`--status`) instead.
- Extra keys appear in `list`/`show --json` under an `extra` object, and are queryable over GraphQL.
- This is metadata, not a new hierarchy level: use it for cross-cutting groupings (like a release) that don't map onto the parent chain.

{{if .RequiredFields}}
## Required Fields on Status Transitions

This project's config file requires the following front matter field(s) before an item can enter a given status. `lif complete`/`lif update`/`lif create` reject an unqualified transition into a listed status with a `POLICY_VIOLATION` error naming what's missing.
{{range .RequiredFields}}
- **{{.Status}}**: {{range $i, $f := .Fields}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}`{{$f}}`{{end}}
{{- end}}
{{if .CommitFieldGated}}
`{{.CommitField}}` is resolved automatically -- pass a git ref and lif records the full 40-character SHA in one write:

```bash
{{if .CompletionCommitGated}}lif complete <id> --commit <ref> --summary "what was done"
{{end}}lif update <id> --set {{.CommitField}}=<ref> ...   # combine with whichever status change requires it
lif create --json "Title" -t task --set {{.CommitField}}=<ref> ...
```

`<ref>` accepts anything `git rev-parse --verify <ref>^{commit}` resolves (`HEAD`, a branch, tag, short SHA); an unresolvable ref fails before any write, and no partial state is saved.
{{end}}
Any other required field needs a plain `--set <field>=<value>` in the same write as the status change.

Surface every policy violation across the workspace: `lif check --strict` (warnings show by default; `--strict` counts them as issues and exits non-zero). An item already sitting in a gated status without the field (e.g. pre-policy data) can still receive maintenance writes -- rename, body edits -- as long as its status doesn't change; the policy only gates the transition itself.
{{end}}

## Review Findings and Attachments

`attachments/<item-id>/` inside the store directory (`lif path` prints where that is) carries the files that belong to one item: machine evidence during the work -- findings documents, diffs, guard drafts -- and, on closure, the finished knowledge artifacts of the container itself (`DESIGN`, `REQUIREMENTS`, `DECISIONS`, `PLAN`). While a topic is still being worked, those documents live in the workspace's working folder and are edited there; closing the container moves them into its attachment directory, keyed by the container's item ID rather than by a folder slug, so the path stays valid for as long as the item does. Attach to the container, not to a leaf -- a design document belongs to the tree, and the container is the one item that spans its whole lifecycle.

No front matter key lists attachments. The directory is already keyed by the item ID, so `lif show` derives the list from it and names the files in the terminal views; a key would be a second list of the same files that goes stale the first time one is added outside the CLI. There is no read verb and no read flag -- the paths are stable and an editor opens them. No check finding, write-time guard, or schema constraint enforces what goes into the directory -- it holds by agreement only; `lif check` reports an attachment directory whose item no longer resolves, or, if the directory itself cannot be read, that read failure.

Promote selected findings from a review artifact into items:

```bash
lif promote <artifact> <finding-id> [<finding-id>...]
```

Every item it creates carries `review=<artifact-path>` (the artifact path exactly as given on the command line) and `finding=<finding-id>` as extra front matter -- the pair a rerun checks before creating a duplicate, and the pair `lif list --where review=<path>` queries to audit everything promoted from one artifact.

`lif rename` carries an item's attachment directory along under all three ID-changing modes -- `lif rename <id> <new-id>`, `lif rename <id> --suffix <suffix>`, and `lif rename --prefix <prefix>` -- and shows the move in `--dry-run` output as `attachments: <old-path> -> <new-path>`. Archiving an item leaves its attachment directory in place: archiving does not change the item's ID, so the directory it is keyed by stays valid -- `lif archive` itself is a batch verb with no `<id>` argument, moving every item already in an archive status to `archive/` inside the store directory in one call.

`lif delete` removes the attachment directory together with the item, and names the files it is about to destroy in the confirmation prompt. Leaving the directory behind would turn every deletion into a `lif check` orphan finding, and under this sink those files are the container's documents rather than a review JSON -- git holds their history.

## Manual Ordering

Items carry an optional `order` field (a fractional-index string) for manual sequencing among siblings under the same parent.

```bash
# Place an item relative to its siblings
lif order <id> --after <sibling-id>   # directly after a sibling that already has an order
lif order <id> --before <sibling-id>  # directly before a sibling that already has an order
lif order <id> --first                # before every sibling
lif order <id> --last                 # after every sibling

# List in manual order (siblings without an order sort last)
lif list --json --sort order

# Set an explicit order value at creation time
lif create --json "Title" -t task --order V
```

- Ordering is scoped per parent: it only compares siblings that share the same `parent`.
- Placing an item relative to (`--after`/`--before`) a sibling that has no order value yet fails -- place that sibling first (e.g. with `--last`).
- A move writes exactly one file: the item being placed, never its neighbours.

## Display Configuration

The config file can carry a `display:` section that controls terminal rendering across every command:

```yaml
display:
  theme: mocha      # latte | frappe | macchiato | mocha -- default: mocha
  max_width: 110    # unset (or 0) yields 110; -1 disables the cap
```

`theme` picks one of the four bundled Catppuccin flavours and applies to all rendered output (badges, tree lines, IDs) for the whole process. `max_width` caps how wide `list`, `milestones`, `roadmap` and `show` render on a terminal; it has no effect on `--json` output or on piped output.

## Output Flags

Terminal output is arranged in one of two forms, and which one you get is the caller's choice, not a property of the command:

| Flag | Commands | Values | Default |
|------|----------|--------|---------|
| `--view` | `list`, `milestones`, `roadmap` | `table` (flat, has a header, sortable) or `tree` (nested, no header) | `table` for `list` and `milestones`, `tree` for `roadmap` |
| `--max-width` | `list`, `milestones`, `roadmap` | columns; `0` disables the cap | unset -- falls back to `display.max_width`, else 110 |
| `--format` | `roadmap` only | `tty` or `markdown` | unset -- detected from whether stdout is a terminal |
| `--tags` | `list`, `milestones`, `roadmap` | boolean | off |
| `--depth` | `roadmap` only | levels below the roadmap root; `0` means no limit | `0` |

`table` drops the hierarchy rather than hiding it, because a tree you can re-sort would be lying about what its indentation means. Use `--parent` to narrow the scope, or `--view tree` when you need to see what belongs to what.

Width resolution, in order: an explicit `--max-width` wins over `display.max_width`, which wins over the built-in 110. Whatever that yields is then narrowed to the detected terminal width, and finally floored at 80 columns -- below that no layout stays readable, so a very narrow terminal gets an 80-column render that overflows rather than an unreadable one that fits. `--max-width 0` and `display.max_width: -1` both mean "no cap": use the terminal's own width, whatever it is.

`--format` matters because `lif roadmap` in a pipe is an artefact generator: it writes GitHub-flavoured Markdown with badge images and links, which is the right output for a file and the wrong output for a terminal. Detection handles the normal case; pass `--format` explicitly when you are generating a file from an interactive shell, or want to see the terminal rendering while piping.

## Failure Output

Every command except `lif run` exits 1 on failure and describes it in exactly one place — which
place depends on whether you passed `--json`. `lif run` exits with the exit code of the failing step.

Without `--json`, two lines go to stderr and stdout stays empty (`lif run` prints one line, `Error: task "<name>": step <n> of <total> failed (exit code <c>)`, and the output of the steps is not suppressed):

```
Error: parent item not found: item-nope
Run '<command> --help' for usage.
```

With `--json`, one document goes to stdout and stderr stays empty:

```json
{"success": false, "error": "parent item not found: item-nope", "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR"}
```

Codes: `NOT_FOUND`, `NO_LIF_DIR`, `INVALID_STATUS`, `FILE_ERROR`,
`VALIDATION_ERROR`, `CONFLICT`, `POLICY_VIOLATION`.

So: under `--json`, parse stdout and branch on the presence of an `error` key;
an empty stderr is normal and not a sign that something went missing. A success
payload never carries `error`, and a failure always does, so the two are
unambiguous. Do not branch on `success` — a successful mutation returns the bare
item document (or a bare array), which carries no `success` key at all; only the
failure document, `check`, and the message-only verbs (`init`, `archive`, `next`
with no match) still have one. No command prints a usage block on failure — if you want the
flags, ask for them with `--help`.

A batch verb (`complete`, `scrap`, `start`, `tag`, `delete`) that fails partway
through returns `{"success": false, "items": [...], "count": n, ...}` — the
items listed there were already written before the failure struck.

## Concurrency Control

Use etags with `--if-match`:
```bash
ETAG=$(lif show <id> --etag-only)
lif update <id> --if-match "$ETAG" ...
```

On conflict, returns an error with the current etag.

## GraphQL Queries

The `lif graphql` command (alias `query`) allows advanced querying using GraphQL.

- Fetch exactly the fields you need, across a potentially large set of items
- Directly read all fields (including `body`) and relationships
- Traverse relationships in a single query
- Execute mutations to create and update items
- `lif graphql --help` for syntax and usage details
- `lif graphql --schema` to view the full GraphQL schema

```bash
# Get all actionable items with their details
lif graphql --json '{ items(filter: { excludeStatus: ["completed", "scrapped"], isBlocked: false }) { id title status type body } }'

# Get a single item with its relationships
lif graphql --json '{ item(id: "item-abc") { title body parent { title } children { id title status } } }'

# Find high-priority bugs
lif graphql --json '{ items(filter: { type: ["bug"], priority: ["critical", "high"] }) { id title } }'

# Search with text
lif graphql --json '{ items(filter: { search: "authentication" }) { id title body } }'
```
